WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:08.320
New Yorker writer Jane Mayer gave an interesting&nbsp;
interview to NPR's Terry Gross a few days ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.760
And the topic was whether Donald Trump might&nbsp;
end up being the first president to ever be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.760
criminally charged. Now, as I've said before,&nbsp;
I maintain and believe that Donald Trump will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:25.920
never see the inside of a prison cell. Some people&nbsp;
write to me and they tell me I'm wrong. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.720
I mean, we can't say I'm wrong yet. It hasn't&nbsp;
happened yet. Some people write to me and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.960
David, when you say that it makes it less likely&nbsp;
Trump will go to prison, I don't think me saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.760
it makes it any less likely. But the fact that&nbsp;
I don't think Trump will ever see the inside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.160
a prison cell doesn't mean he won't be charged.&nbsp;
And as Jane Mayer points out in this interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.360
with NPR, which you can find online, there are&nbsp;
a number of viable paths to Donald Trump ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.000
up with criminal charges against him being the&nbsp;
first president to have such charges against him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.400
Right now, we know of two possible criminal&nbsp;
charges, investigations with possible criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.800
charges coming. The first is the investigation&nbsp;
in Georgia over Donald Trump's now known multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:13.040
phone calls trying to get the election results&nbsp;
changed in the 2020 Georgia presidential election.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.320
The second one and the one that actually predates&nbsp;
the Georgia probe is the one led by Manhattan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:24.400
District Attorney Cyrus Vance, which is looking&nbsp;
at Trump's financial practices and in that one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:32.000
tax banking, insurance fraud all on the table. And&nbsp;
that's the one where they finally got a decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.000
from the Supreme Court that indeed Trump's&nbsp;
financial records must be turned over and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.080
they have been. So now Cyrus Vance has those&nbsp;
records. Now, as I've said before, the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:48.800
likely scenario is some kind of financial charges,&nbsp;
probably against others at the Trump Organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.920
But still, it's possible Donald Trump could be&nbsp;
criminally charged in that investigation. Jane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:02.480
Mayer addresses many of the important questions.&nbsp;
For example, Terry Gross asks her whether even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:08.560
if Trump committed crimes, we should just move on&nbsp;
because Trump is gone. And Jane Mayer points out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.000
listen, it's true that lots of the countries&nbsp;
that charge former leaders with crimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.440
are not necessarily the countries we're&nbsp;
trying to emulate. But she argues that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.000
the crimes Trump committed aren't even related&nbsp;
to the presidency, these are business crimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.280
for example. What reason is there not to pursue&nbsp;
to pursue them? And this is an important point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.400
We know that every president makes decisions that&nbsp;
lead to deaths, but we don't charge presidents in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.920
those cases because it would make being&nbsp;
president impossible. I'm not saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.120
that I'm against war crimes charges. That's not at&nbsp;
all what I'm saying. But every time you cut aid or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.800
shift military resources, send troops into war,&nbsp;
change some welfare program to a drone strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.760
every presidency leads to civilian deaths, some&nbsp;
of which could have been avoided. Right or wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.560
U.S. presidents don't get charged with that stuff&nbsp;
and we can have a debate about that. But that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.840
not what we're talking about here. If we find out&nbsp;
Trump was laundering money through his business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:10.640
who cares if he was president for a four year&nbsp;
period? It's unrelated to him being president.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.160
And there shouldn't be a culture of impunity.&nbsp;
As Jane Mayer correctly points out, the possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:21.680
financial crimes and fraud in New York are&nbsp;
significant, taking tax deductions on things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.240
that aren't tax deductible to the tune of millions&nbsp;
of dollars, including even some of those hush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:32.880
money payments that Donald Trump made exaggerating&nbsp;
or diminishing the claimed value of real estate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.480
to manipulate lenders and taxation authorities&nbsp;
to his benefit. There's a very long list here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:44.080
and these are crimes in the state of New York on&nbsp;
the. End of this, the question of prison comes up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:52.480
are the things being investigated theoretically&nbsp;
punishable by prison time? Yes. Would Trump get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:04:00.160
prison time even if found guilty? It's possible.&nbsp;
Would he serve it? I can't imagine. Jane Mayer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:05.520
says in the interview she believes Trump&nbsp;
would flee the country if he actually were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.640
sentenced to prison time. The truth&nbsp;
is, I don't think that's very likely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.560
but we're in such unprecedented territory that&nbsp;
it's very difficult to predict. My sense is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:22.000
most likely scenario is there's no prison time&nbsp;
and Trump doesn't have to flee. I still believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.840
that's the most obvious and logical scenario.&nbsp;
The importance to me of criminally charging Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.400
isn't to put him in prison. It's twofold. First,&nbsp;
it's to show that a president isn't above the law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.720
and maybe that will prevent another president from&nbsp;
being as corrupt and criminal as Donald Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:42.240
Maybe that's naive, but that's&nbsp;
one important element of this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.080
Secondly, as a very practical matter, if there&nbsp;
are criminal charges against Donald Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.560
it could just keep him too busy to even think&nbsp;
about running again in twenty, twenty four. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.440
that would be a very good thing. We'll have&nbsp;
more coverage of this on The David Pakman Show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:03.680
Instagram. You can follow us there at David Pakman&nbsp;
show. Follow me on Instagram at David Pakman if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:11.920
you want to see me. What would be the right word?&nbsp;
And that's not really accosted, smothered by a dog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:18.320
that doesn't understand the concept of personal&nbsp;
space. Follow me on Instagram at David Pakman.

